window.docContents[146].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Open body A. HalfOf π b adds nominal pair-and-body identity to the upstream\nleft/right Half. OpenBody π b records per-half mass and one position coordinate. The\nexisting Assembles rigidBodyS massK license produces the whole-body total and its\nmagnitude reads the two real fields. Upstream's union theorems state the corresponding\nmass and inertia-tensor laws. Same-name records share bearers, while P and Q do not:\n\n\n\nThe HCP total is the narrow restatement: upstream union_mass and\nunion_inertiaTensor are about PhysLib's RigidBody, not the nominal\nHalfOf .P .A bearer.\n\n2. Centre of mass is a weighted mean. Upstream supplies both the rigid-body union law\nand the general weighted-mean machinery. HCP's centerOfMassCarving uses this state's\nOpenBody.mass for its weights, and centerOfMass_eq gives the resulting formula. For\nexample, masses 3 and 5 at positions 0 and 10 give 25/4 (6.25), not the unit-weight\nmidpoint 5. HCP's position quantities use the same nominal half bearers as mass, but an\nattempted sum has no Assembles license:\n\n\n\nThe physical content is\n(mA + mB) com = mA comA + mB comB, not com = comA + comB.\n\n3. The degenerate whole. PhysLib defines the massless body's centre of mass to be the\norigin by convention. The license to build centerOfMassCarving is hypothesis h, that\nthe state's total mass is nonzero; a massless state cannot satisfy it, so it cannot build\nthe carving:\n\n\n\n4. Angular velocity is intensive. The two rigid halves share one angular velocity;\nadding their measurements would double it. The upstream finding proves the intensive law\nand refutes extensivity, while the HCP attempted assembly is rejected:\n\n\n\n5. Upstream frames. PhysLib's bare rotational-energy API accepts the lab-frame\nM.angularVelocity t against the body-fixed inertia tensor. The term below type-checks,\nbut it is physically wrong:\n\n\n\n6. PKC frames. rotationalContraction retains the frame in the type. Lab-frame ω is\ntherefore refused, body-frame ω is accepted, and the bridge theorem equates the accepted\ncomponents with twice PhysLib's own rotational kinetic energy:\n\n\n\n7. Homogeneity returns as a special case. For a nonzero-radius solid sphere, the proved\ninertia tensor is (2/5) m R² times the identity matrix:\n\n\n\nPhysLib records this target as informal_lemma CM-RB-sphereI. The proof in\nForPhysLib.ClassicalMechanics.SolidSphereInertia is therefore a ready first upstream\ncontribution for the PhysLib contributor. L4 next revisits motion along one line seen by\none observer.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.7. Lesson","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Lesson"},"/#annotation-pkc-math-symbol":{"contents":"Overrides every naming heuristic for one token.\n\nattribute [pkc_math_symbol \"n_{d}\"]  MironovNK.nd\nattribute [pkc_math_symbol \"m_{vt}\"] MironovNK.mvt\n\n\nThis is not always cosmetic. The rendering heuristic maps a Greek-letter spelling to its letter, so\na refractive-index field named nu would typeset as  — an angular frequency. Notation stated\nonce, on the field, wins over every heuristic, and the index below is therefore a table of the places\nwhere a heuristic was deliberately overruled.\n\n* Declaration* Renders as* Layout* avsForward* \\sigma^0* function head* byPolarization* P* function head* columns* J* function head* combined* D* function head* decay* \\tau* function head* grad* \\nabla* prefix operator* letExample* q* function head* model* F* function head* noted* \\gamma* function head* residual* R* function head* scaled* g* function head* scaledByTwo* u* function head* shared* S* function head* twiceBound* T* function head\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009How a definition reads — the rendering family","header":"33.8.2. @[pkc_math_symbol]  — fix the notation","id":"/#annotation-pkc-math-symbol"}});